Ah, I missed that. Now it makes sense (as much sense as a unix system
with an incorrect system clock can make).

Well, apart from hitting the sysadmin with a wand of enlightenment
there's another easy solution.

Jukka posts with Gnus and you can configure everything with Gnus.


This snippet of Emacs lisp inserts a date header that lies one hour
into the future (the correct time for everything else but Jukkas ISPs
shell machine):

(defun bhaak-message-add-correct-date ()
	"Add a Date header that is one hour in the future"
  (message-add-header (concat "Date: " (message-make-date (time-subtract (current-time) '(0 -3600 0))))))
(add-hook 'message-send-hook 'bhaak-message-add-correct-date)

